{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01KpZ5hPfrLMPjBrtLbR8NXm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Here's what was written for **Q17: Planning failures hardest to detect in production?**\n\n---\n\n**Key content in `03-017-planning-failures-hardest-to-detect-in-production.md`:**\n\nThe answer centers on a **4-class taxonomy of semantic failures** that are operationally invisible:\n\n| Failure class | Detection mechanism |\n|---|---|\n| **Goal drift** | Embed original goal; track cosine distance per step (>0.15 → alert) |\n| **Sycophantic looping** | Hash `(action, tool, args)` tuple; identical fingerprint in last 3 steps → hard stop |\n| **Silent tool success / semantic failure** | HTTP 200 doesn't mean correct data; validate output schema + spot-sample embeddings |\n| **Over-decomposition** | All sub-steps succeed but high-level goal unmet; only caught by end-to-end acceptance test on final state |\n\nThe **core insight** that separates strong candidates: operational health metrics (no exceptions, tool calls succeeding) are insufficient — you need semantic progress signals. The answer includes a concrete LangSmith/OpenTelemetry span schema and a cost vs. coverage tradeoff for each detection approach.\n\n**Pitfalls flagged:** Candidates who only describe crashes/timeouts as \"hard to detect,\" or who say \"just add logging\" without specifying what signals to log."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":367,"cache_read_input_tokens":62875,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":367,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b413989e-97a2-4b93-be9f-d91508644fef","uuid":"1b32559d-d287-4cd3-8f7f-c05885566eae"}
